  and ../TestResults/TestRuntimeOptimizations/DrawAttribution/20261104T143728Z/.

Numerical class-fixture rollout (2026-11-04; initial three-suite batch qualified)
  Preserve every original case, numerical reference, assertion, tolerance and sample. Retain fresh
  case-owned scene textures and cameras, with complete packed-input and borrowed-reference resets
  for each exclusive lease. Owners remain local to one test class; there is no global live-program
  cache, GPU concurrency or process split. Class fixtures must initialize before per-case platform
  scopes and retire before the collection context. Cold and lifecycle subjects remain isolated.
  The matched pre-change Release selections pass 56/56 with retained caches: shallow continuity
  7 cases in 26.044s, UV 37 cases in 24.198s, pixel-normal 12 cases in 12.576s. These are separate
  process walls, including startup and teardown. Sources, test DLL, 909 shader modules and the
  434-entry executable cache remained unchanged.
  Corrected-source builds pass in 19.265s Debug / 58.757s Release. An earlier Debug build failed
  on a missing generic closing bracket in the shallow fixture factory; it ran no tests and its
  separate receipts are retained. The original 56 cases pass in each configuration with zero
  name or numerical-output differences. Candidate per-class walls are 13.486s / 11.831s for
  shallow continuity, 9.024s / 7.485s for UV, and 8.621s / 6.618s for pixel-normal (Debug / Release).
  Supporting selections pass 30/30 in 13.072s / 9.942s: the original 17 budget cases, the existing
  budget isolation control, three new separate fixture controls and nine explicit cold mechanics
  checks. These are 86 distinct focused cases per configuration, zero failures/skips. Discovery
  is 5,950 per configuration: exactly three new controls and no removals from the pilot candidate.
  All 14 rollout source hashes and both test DLLs remain stable; 909 shader modules per configuration
  match baseline. Release retains the same 434 valid cache entries and 17,523,863 bytes; Debug has
  five valid entries and 708,591 bytes. Source and scoped raw-result reviews are clean.
  One complete warmed Release AllTests run, with the retained cache and no intervening build,
  passes 5,949 with zero failures and seven retained skips in 574.267s (9m34.3s). All 5,952 prior
  full-run identities remain; four fixture controls are added (the budget pilot plus this batch's
  three). There are 5,956 unique runtime rows and 5,950 adapter identities; unchanged dynamic Poisson
  expansion accounts for the six-row difference. The nine explicit cold checks, real-GC stress
  and all four fixture controls pass. Skip identities and reasons exactly match the prior warm run.
  The 14 rollout sources, test DLL and 909 Release shader modules remain unchanged through this
  full run. The cache remains valid at 434 entries and 17,523,863 bytes. The command excludes builds
  and includes stress. No fresh full Debug run is claimed. The earlier warmed 641.200s sample
  predates both the budget pilot and this rollout; the shorter current sample does not isolate
  either change or establish a stable percentage. The routine five-minute target remains unmet.
  Remaining numerical cost leaders in this run include WaterRefractionTests (33 rows / 19.236s),
  AmbientOcclusionCompositeTests (6 / 12.848s), LumOnDirectWorldProbeVisibilityTests (62 / 11.420s)
  and AmbientOcclusionHorizonBehaviorTests (10 / 9.896s). These are summed case durations, not
  predicted wall savings or proof of safe sharing. Larger mixed runtime/publication/ownership
  subjects require method-level classification before reuse; their lifecycle guarantees remain.
  Receipts: ../TestResults/TestRuntimeOptimizations/NumericalClassFixtureRollout/20261104T154339Z/.

Runtime-boundary audit and further numerical reuse (2026-11-04; AO batch qualified)
  The user authorized the next audit and numerical reuse batch. SurfaceLightingConsumerRuntimeTests
  and SurfaceLightingSpatialRuntimeTests retain per-case runtime/program owners. The consumer
  methods exercise worker retries, upload admission, publication, obsolete completions, recreation
  and callback disposal. Spatial methods exercise temporal anchors/history, physical ring retention,
  signed streaming generations, dark-room publication and mixed-consumer replacement/invalidation.
  SurfaceLightingConsumerRuntimeFixture creates its API-owned production shader library before
  registered renderers demand programs; RuntimeLightingPrograms.Initialize asserts initially empty
  demand state. Teardown stops consumers before disposing the library and its cache/platform
  dependencies. No independent numerical reset/lease boundary was found: sharing these live owners
  would cross API, callback, configuration and publication lifetimes. Existing validated executable
  byte caching remains available. The audit recommends no changes to these two runtime families
  under the numerical-only reuse authorization; a broader lifetime redesign is outside this batch.
  Evidence: GPU/SurfaceLightingConsumerRuntimeTests.cs (registered lifetime, worker, query and
  disposal cases), GPU/SurfaceLightingSpatialRuntimeTests.cs (camera/ring/streaming/mixed cases),
  GPU/Fixtures/SurfaceLightingConsumerRuntimeFixture.cs and GPU/Fixtures/RuntimeLightingPrograms.cs,
  all paths relative to ../VanillaGraphicsExpanded.Tests/.
  The fresh pre-change Release baseline passes 120/120 with no failures/skips in separate retained-
  cache processes: consumer runtime 13 / 35.289s; spatial runtime 6 / 19.955s; water refraction
  33 / 29.600s; AO composite 6 / 12.286s; direct world-probe visibility 62 / 15.412s. Sources, DLL,
  all 909 shader modules and the valid 434-entry cache remain unchanged.
  AO composite is the narrow implementation: each row repeats VerifyComposition with
  the same compile options. Reuse one program within the existing case-owned Programs lifetime,
  passing it to each helper call and clearing borrowed sampler/camera references before scenario
  resources retire. All six rows, numerical sweeps, draw order, references and tolerances remain.
  No class-scoped production owner or new production reset API is needed. Source-derived program
  creations fall from 96 to six: three single-scenario rows plus three rows of 31 scenarios.
  All 96 VerifyComposition calls and their 384 draws remain. These are source-derived counts,
  not instrumented compilation or driver-work savings. Independent source review is clean.
  Canonical Debug/Release builds pass in 108.887s / 100.694s. Dedicated AO selections pass 6/6
  in 9.985s / 6.491s, with unchanged case identities and output; the matched Release baseline is
  12.286s. Separate regression processes pass 166/166 in 99.562s / 75.882s: the other 114 baseline
  cases plus 52 related AO checks. These are 172 distinct passing checks per configuration, zero
  failures/skips. Both regression selections retain two spatial MixedConsumers diagnostic-output
  differences from the standalone Release controls, confined to printed minRGB values. Yaw samples,
  threshold/replacement decisions, ray counts and asserted outcomes remain unchanged. The changed
  AO methods do not execute in those regression processes. Raw differences remain in the receipts;
  no exact regression-stdout equivalence is claimed across these differently grouped runs.
  Discovery remains 5,950 per configuration, with no cases added or removed. The sole changed test
  source and both test DLLs remain stable; all 909 shader modules per configuration match baseline.
  The Release cache remains valid at 434 entries and 17,523,863 bytes. The dedicated timing pair
  supports the local result, not a stable percentage or whole-suite speedup. No complete suite is
  rerun for this test-only batch: the last complete warmed Release measurement, 574.267s, predates
  it. No fresh full Debug/Release or under-five-minute result is established by these scoped checks.
  WaterRefractionTests executes its comparison once per ordinary case. Cross-case sharing would
  require persistent API/asset composition, immutable compile variants (quality, resolution and
  capture paths), and reset of three input buffers, 12 samplers and camera/lighting/fog/material
  state; diagnostic methods also use explicit per-case asset overrides. Leave this family unchanged.
  DirectWorldProbeVisibilityTestBase already caches each compiled variant within its test instance.
  Cross-case reuse would require a class-owned identity/define dictionary and complete resets for
  debug, atlas-gather and SH9-gather bindings, near-field staging resources, frame/world publications
  and case-owned LightingResources. No narrow existing reset seam was found; leave it unchanged.
  These are limits of the current narrow reuse design, not proof that broader optimization is
  impossible. Preserve their fresh owners rather than changing production lifecycle contracts.
  Receipts: ../TestResults/TestRuntimeOptimizations/NextNumericalReuseBatch/20261104T165742Z/.
